<% content_for :title, "#{t('legal.privacy.title')} · #{t('app_name')}" %>
<% content_for :description, t("legal.privacy.meta_description") %>

<main id="main" class="spread py-12">
  <%= render "legal/head",
        eyebrow: t("legal.eyebrow.privacy"),
        title: t("legal.privacy.title"),
        updated_on: LegalController::LAST_UPDATED %>

  <article class="legal-doc">
    <p class="lede">
      This notice explains what personal data is processed when you use
      aperto.wine — whether you are a guest scanning a table QR code or a
      restaurateur managing a wine list — under Articles 13 and 14 of
      Regulation (EU) 2016/679 (GDPR).
    </p>

    <h2>Two distinct roles</h2>
    <p>
      aperto.wine is a technical service restaurants use to publish their wine
      list and receive table orders. That creates two different situations, and
      the distinction matters because it changes who you should approach:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Order and table data.</strong> The <strong>restaurant is the
        data controller</strong>: it decides to collect this data, uses it to
        serve the table, and sets how long it is kept. aperto.wine acts as a
        <strong>processor</strong> under Article 28 GDPR — it processes that
        data only on the restaurant's behalf and on its instructions. The
        clauses of that appointment are in the
        <%= link_to "Terms of Service", terms_path %>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Restaurateur accounts.</strong> Here the data controller is the
        operator of aperto.wine, identified below: the account exists to provide
        the service to whoever subscribes to it.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What we collect from guests</h2>
    <p>
      Reading a wine list requires no account, no app and no details at all.
      Data appears only when you send an order:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>the guest name you type</strong> so the staff can find you — a
        nickname is fine, the field is for the dining room and is never
        verified;
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>the table</strong> you are ordering from, derived from the QR
        code you scanned, and the <strong>order lines</strong> with their
        quantities;
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>the cart in progress</strong>, which stays in your browser
        session and is never stored as an order until you send it;
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>a signed technical cookie lasting 24 hours</strong>, holding
        only the reference codes of orders sent from that device, so the device
        that placed an order can see its status;
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>server logs</strong> (IP address, timestamp, requested page),
        generated automatically and used for security and diagnostics.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">
      We do not ask for an email address, a phone number, payment details or
      location data. Payment happens at the table, outside this service.
    </p>

    <h2>What we collect from restaurateurs</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Account data:</strong> name, email address and password (stored
        only as a hash, never in clear text).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Venue data:</strong> name, address, contact details published on
        the menu, tables and the QR codes generated for them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Content you enter:</strong> wines, prices, vintages, tasting
        notes, lists and the orders you receive.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why we process it, and on what legal basis</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Providing the service</strong> — showing the menu, holding the
        cart, passing the order to the venue, running the account: performance
        of a contract or pre-contractual steps (Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Keeping the service safe</strong> — preventing abuse and
        automated ordering, keeping technical logs: legitimate interests
        (Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Legal obligations</strong> — where a rule requires us to retain
        or disclose data: Art. 6(1)(c) GDPR.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      We do not profile people, we take no automated decisions about them, and
      we do not pass data to third parties for marketing.
    </p>

    <h2>Cookies and similar technologies</h2>
    <p>
      aperto.wine uses <strong>technical cookies and storage only</strong>: the
      session cookie navigation needs to work, the cart held in that session,
      the 24-hour signed cookie that remembers orders sent from the device, and
      your language preference. None of them profiles anyone or tracks people
      across sites.
    </p>
    <p>
      We use no third-party analytics, no advertising pixels, no social plugins
      and no advertising tracking of any kind. That is why you see no consent
      banner: under the Italian supervisory authority's cookie guidelines of
      10 June 2021, purely technical tools do not require one. If we ever
      introduce non-technical tools we will ask for consent <em>before</em>
      setting them, and refusing will be as easy as accepting.
    </p>

    <h2>Who we share data with</h2>
    <p>
      Data is never sold or published. It is accessible to the staff of the
      restaurant concerned and to the providers that help us run the service,
      each appointed as a processor and bound by contract:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-4">
      <table class="ledger-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col"><%= t("legal.subprocessors.name") %></th>
            <th scope="col"><%= t("legal.subprocessors.role") %></th>
            <th scope="col"><%= t("legal.subprocessors.location") %></th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Render Services, Inc.</td>
            <td>Application and database hosting</td>
            <td>Frankfurt, Germany (EU)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Komoot GmbH — Photon</td>
            <td>
              Address suggestions in the management area, on OpenStreetMap data.
              It receives only the text a restaurateur types into the address
              field: no guest or order data.
            </td>
            <td>Germany (EU)</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      As at the date of this notice <strong>no email provider is
      engaged</strong>: the service sends no email. We will update this list
      before it sends any. Address suggestions derive from OpenStreetMap data,
      © OpenStreetMap contributors.
    </p>

    <h2>Where the data is held</h2>
    <p>
      The application and its database are hosted in the Frankfurt (Germany)
      region, inside the European Union. We make no transfers of personal data
      to third countries. Should that change, we will put in place one of the
      safeguards in Chapter V GDPR and update this notice.
    </p>

    <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Orders and table data:</strong> for as long as the restaurant,
        as controller, decides, and in any case no longer than its relationship
        with aperto.wine; when the account closes the data is deleted or
        returned on its instructions.
      </li>
      <li><strong>The device's order cookie:</strong> 24 hours.</li>
      <li><strong>The cart:</strong> for the life of the browser session.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Restaurateur account and content:</strong> while the account is
        active, and afterwards only as long as legal obligations require.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Server logs:</strong> for as long as security and diagnostics
        require.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      You may at any time request access to your data, its rectification or
      erasure, restriction of processing, portability, and you may object to
      processing based on legitimate interests (Arts. 15–22 GDPR).
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Who to ask:</strong> for the data in an order or a table, address
      the request to the <strong>restaurant</strong>, which is the controller —
      we assist it in answering. For a restaurateur account, or if you are not
      sure who to approach, write to the address in the legal information below.
    </p>
    <p>
      You may always lodge a complaint with the Italian supervisory authority,
      the <strong>Garante per la protezione dei dati personali</strong>
      (Piazza Venezia 11, 00187 Rome —
      <a href="https://www.garanteprivacy.it" rel="noopener noreferrer">garanteprivacy.it</a>),
      or bring proceedings before the courts.
    </p>

    <h2>Minors</h2>
    <p>
      The service concerns alcoholic drinks and is not intended for anyone under
      18. We do not knowingly collect data from minors. Verifying the age of
      whoever drinks remains with the restaurant, which does so at the table as
      Article 14-ter of Law 125/2001 requires.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
      This notice may be updated; the date at the top of the page marks the
      current version. Substantive changes affecting restaurateurs are also
      announced through the account.
    </p>

    <%= render "legal/operator_identity" %>
  </article>

  <%= render "legal/foot",
        other_document_path: terms_path,
        other_document_label: t("legal.terms.title") %>
</main>
